Two Raspberry Pi Picos Talking with UART
What UART is
UART lets two Picos send text to each other over 3 wires
UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver-Transmitter) is a serial connection: data travels one bit at a time down a single wire. Each board has a TX (transmit) pin to talk and an RX (receive) pin to listen.
What you need
2 Raspberry Pi Picos
2 micro-USB cables (one per Pico)
3 jumper wires
Wiring
Connect the boards with 3 wires: TX crosses to RX in both directions, and the grounds connect.
Pico A (sender)	Wire	Pico B (receiver)
TX Pin (GP0)	→	RX Pin (GP1)
RX Pin (GP1)	←	TX Pin (GP0)
"GND, physical pin 3"	—	"GND, physical pin 3"

Rule 1: TX crosses to RX. One board's "talk" pin goes to the other board's "listen" pin. 
Rule 2: always connect the grounds. The ground wire matters even if things seem to work without it.
Do not connect the 3V3 or VBUS pins together when both boards have their own USB power. Only connect GND and the two signal wires.
